# Building Custom Nav Menus with `wp_get_nav_menu_items` and Nested Hierarchies

When you need to customize the output of a WordPress navigation menu, relying solely on `wp_nav_menu()` often hits a wall. That function generates clean HTML5 semantics, but it lacks granular control over attributes like specific classes or custom content injection between links. If your requirement involves injecting a description (excerpt) alongside the title and maintaining a nested sub-menu structure, you must move to manual rendering using `wp_get_nav_menu_items()`.

The core challenge lies in detecting hierarchy. The `WP_Nav_Menu_Item` object returned by `wp_get_nav_menu_items()` contains a property called `$item->menu_item_parent`. This integer holds the ID of the parent item if one exists; otherwise, it is zero or null. By iterating through the top-level items first and then recursively fetching their children, you can reconstruct the nested `<ul>` structure required for dropdowns.

Here is how to approach generating the specific HTML structure you described. You will need to separate your logic into two distinct loops: one for the main menu level and one for rendering sub-menus.

First, retrieve the menu object and its items. Note that we must explicitly set the `order` parameter to ensure consistent output, and we often need to filter out empty items.

```php
$menu_name = 'main-nav';
$locations = get_nav_menu_locations();
$menu_id = $locations[$menu_name]; // Get the ID of the assigned menu location

if ( ! $menu_id ) {
    return; // Handle case where menu is not assigned to a location
}

$menu_items = wp_get_nav_menu_items( $menu_id, [ 'order' => 'ASC' ] );

// Filter out empty items if necessary
$menu_items = array_filter( $menu_items, function( $item ) {
    return ! is_null( $item ) && $item->ID !== '';
} );

foreach ( $menu_items as $top_level_item ) :

    // Determine the link and content for the main item
    // We assume the custom logic involving get_page() for excerpts here
    $link = get_permalink( $top_level_item->object_id ); 
    // If using custom post types or external links, adjust accordingly

    echo '<li class="item">';

    echo '<a href="' . esc_url( $link ) . '" class="title">' . esc_html( get_the_title( $top_level_item->object_id ) ) . '</a>';
    echo '<a href="' . esc_url( $link ) . '" class="desc">' . esc_html( get_post_field( 'post_excerpt', $top_level_item->object_id ) ) . '</a>';

    // Check if this item has children (sub-menu)
    $children = wp_get_nav_menu_items( $top_level_item->ID, [ 'order' => 'ASC' ] );
    $children = array_filter( $children, function( $child ) { return ! is_null( $child ) && $child->ID !== ''; } );

    if ( ! empty( $children ) ) :
        echo '<ul class="sub-menu">';

        foreach ( $children as $child_item ) :
            // Apply the same logic for child items
            $child_link = get_permalink( $child_item->object_id );

            echo '<li class="item">';
            echo '<a href="' . esc_url( $child_link ) . '" class="title">' . esc_html( get_the_title( $child_item->object_id ) ) . '</a>';
            echo '<a href="' . esc_url( $child_link ) . '" class="desc">' . esc_html( get_post_field( 'post_excerpt', $child_item->object_id ) ) . '</a>';
            echo '</li>';

        endforeach;

        echo '</ul>';
    endif;

    echo '</li>';

endforeach;
```

### Handling the Data Source

In your original code snippet, you were retrieving data using `get_post_meta( $item->ID, '_menu_item_object_id', true )`. This is valid if you are dealing with a specific custom meta setup, but for standard WordPress menus, `$item->object_id` is the direct property holding the ID of the linked object (e.g., a Post, Page, or Custom Post Type). Using `$item->object_id` is more efficient and reliable than querying post meta for the ID itself.

Furthermore, the logic `get_page( $id )` works well for standard pages, but if your menu items link to posts, categories, tags, or external URLs, that approach will fail. The code above uses `$top_level_item->object_id` directly with `get_permalink()` and `get_the_title()`, which is the robust way to handle mixed content types in navigation menus.

### Performance Considerations

A potential pitfall in this recursive approach is the number of database queries. If you have a top-level item with many children, and each child has grandchildren, you are making multiple calls to `wp_get_nav_menu_items()`. While this keeps the logic clean and readable, it can degrade performance on large sites. For most standard themes, however, the overhead is negligible compared to the complexity saved by not hardcoding HTML strings manually.

If you find yourself needing to traverse deep hierarchies frequently, consider caching the menu items using `wp_cache_get()` and `wp_cache_set()`, or utilizing a dedicated navigation library that abstracts this recursion for better performance.
